{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 20, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "**Chasing Cherry Blossoms in Kyoto**\n\nLast spring, I finally made the trip to Japan that I had dreamed of for years. Landing in Tokyo was a whirlwind of neon lights and endless energy, but my heart was set on the ancient capital of Kyoto.\n\nThe moment I stepped onto the train heading west, the pace slowed. In Kyoto, tradition breathes through every cobblestone street. I spent my mornings wandering through the Arashiyama Bamboo Grove, where towering stalks swayed gently in the breeze, creating a natural symphony. The air smelled of damp earth and fresh pine.\n\nNo visit to Japan is complete without experiencing a tea ceremony. In a quiet machiya townhouse, a master whisked matcha with precise, graceful movements. The bitter froth paired perfectly with a sweet wagashi cake, teaching me the beauty of *ichi-go ichi-e*—the idea that this specific moment will never happen again.\n\nThe highlight, however, was Philosopher's Path during peak sakura season. Pink petals drifted down like snow, carpeting the canal below. Locals and tourists alike walked in hushed reverence, capturing photos or simply soaking in the ephemeral beauty. It felt as though time had paused just for us.\n\nEvenings were for exploration. I got lost in the narrow alleys of Pontocho, stumbling upon tiny izakayas serving steaming bowls of ramen and grilled skewers. The warmth of the owners and the clinking of glasses made me feel instantly welcome.\n\nLeaving Japan was bittersweet. The country left an indelible mark on my soul, blending the ultra-modern with the deeply traditional in a way that feels magical. I didn't just visit Japan; I experienced a different way of seeing the world. I'm already planning my return.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.06189906}, {"token": "Ch", "logprob": -0.041959435}, {"token": "asing", "logprob": -1.3659785}, {"token": " Cherry", "logprob": -2.6